  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has fields including `timestamp` (date), `Carrier` (string), `Cancelled` (boolean), `DestCityName`, `OriginCityName`, `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`, and `FlightDelay`. The time field is `timestamp`.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-f5e3bd2a Flights' (id `s4-f5e3bd2a-flights-3d22c7d60cc6`) has exactly two panels:\n   - A line chart of flight count over `timestamp` (date histogram).\n   - A bar chart of flight count split by `Carrier` (top 10 terms).\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-f5e3bd2a Cancelled flights' (id `84089a0e-e1ef-42f9-a0ed-8b8b6fa5ab98`) is an `.es-query` rule on `stackAlerts`.\n   - It runs every 1m and counts documents with `Cancelled: true` over a 5m window on `timestamp`.\n   - It fires when the count is greater than 10.\n   - The rule was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n\n4. **Panel edit and read-back:** The carrier panel is now titled 'Flights by carrier'. I re-read the dashboard and the title change is there. Reading the alert rule back shows `enabled: true`, with status `ok`.\n\nChoices I made: the carrier panel is a bar chart, and the over-time panel is a line chart. The rule's query is a filter on `Cancelled: true`, since the task did not specify one.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
